e. Changing default authenticators prior to first use;
f. Changing or refreshing authenticators [Assignment: organization-defined time period by authenticator type] or when [Assignment: organization-defined events] occur;
g. Protecting authenticator content from unauthorized disclosure and modification;
h. Requiring individuals to take, and having devices implement, specific controls to protect authenticators; and
i. Changing authenticators for group or role accounts when membership to those accounts changes.

appendix.Control Objective:
Luminor must manage and protect authenticators used to verify the identities of users and devices accessing the organization’s information systems. This includes:
Issuing unique authenticators to users and devices;
Ensuring secure storage, usage, and transmission of authenticators;
Regularly updating and revoking authenticators as required to maintain security.

appendix.Functional Requirements:
Manage Authenticators by:
Verify the identity of individuals, groups, or devices before distributing authenticators to ensure authorized access. 
	Authentication mechanisms must be assigned to an individual Account and not shared among multiple Accounts.

	Set up initial authenticator content and require any default authenticators to be changed before first use to prevent unauthorized access. 

Ensure all authenticators meet a defined strength level (e.g., complexity, length) for their intended use and level of access, providing adequate protection against unauthorized access attempts. 
Define intervals for changing or refreshing authenticators based on type, and require updates in response to specific events, such as detected compromise. 

Secure authenticator content by encrypting it during storage and transmission, preventing unauthorized access and modifications. “First-time” and temporary passwords shall be transmitted in an encrypted form via secure channels. If this cannot be fulfilled a temporary password can be transmitted in plain text only separately from the username via a different channel.

Password-Based Authentication: 


	IA-5(1): Password-based Authentication. Implement measures for password security, including a list of prohibited passwords, cryptographically protected transmission and storage, and support for complex passwords and passphrases. Password entry shall be obscured on the user's screen. (e.g., on web forms use the input type "password").

Account password requirements (minimum):
Be at least 12 characters in length;
Contain at least one uppercase alphabetic character (A-Z);
Contain at least one lowercase alphabetic characters (a-z);
Have at least one numerical character (0-9);
Have at least one special character (e.g. ~!@#$%^&*()_-+=);
No password re-use within previous 6 passwords;
Passwords are changed at least every 90 days;
Minimum password age must be set at least for 15 days.
Lock out after 5 failed attempts; The lockout duration must be minimum 30 minutes or until an administrator enables the user Account.
Privileged account password requirements (minimum):
Be at least 17 characters in length;
Contain at least one uppercase alphabetic character (A-Z);
Contain at least one lowercase alphabetic characters (a-z);
Have at least one numerical character (0-9);
Have at least one special character (e.g. ~!@#$%^&*()_-+=);
No password reuse within previous 8 passwords;
Passwords are changed at least every 60 days;
Minimum password age must be set at least for 15 days.
PIN requirements:
Be at least 6 characters in length; 
No PIN reuse within previous 5 codes;
PINs are changed at least every 180 days;
Lock out after 5 failed attempts;
No fixed default PIN code.

Public Key-Based Authentication: 
IA-5(2): Public Key-Based Authentication: Secure access to private keys, map authenticated identities, validate PKI certificates, and maintain local revocation data for public key-based authentication.

Authenticator Revocation and Management Procedures: 
Implement procedures for the distribution, revocation, and replacement of authenticators in cases of loss, compromise, or personnel changes, maintaining secure access controls throughout the lifecycle. 
IA-5(6): Protection of Authenticators: Protect authenticators in line with the highest security level of information they secure, ensuring strong safeguards.
The User’s identity must be verified before modifying any authentication credentials – for example, performing password resets, provisioning new Tokens, or generating new keys.

Authenticator Quality Standards for Biometric Data (if applicable): 
IA-5(12): Biometric Authentication Performance: For biometric-based authentication, ensure mechanisms meet specified quality requirements for accuracy and security.
Password Vaults:
IA-5(18): Password Managers: Employ password managers to generate and store complex passwords, ensuring passwords are encrypted and stored securely. 
Master password used for password vaults shall meet the privileged account password requirements.

